\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The determinant and diagonal bound on the residual edge}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{On the edge of \ref{fdip-012P}, let the class-function
kernel take one value at the identity and a second value at
every other element, and suppose the diagonal matrix below is
invertible. The indicator matrix is the update of
\ref{fdip-013I}, the rescaling is the one in
\ref{fdip-012U}, and the Rayleigh inequality is the hypothesis
of \ref{fdip-012Y}. The determinant and the diagonal bound are
the quantities given by \ref{fdip-013I}.}

\proof{
\p{Let #{h} be an element of the group of \ref{fdip-0126}
distinct from the identity, and write #{\kappa=\mathcal K(1)}
and #{\mu=\mathcal K(h)}. For #{g} in the #{m}-th double
coset, the product #{gx^{-1}} equals the identity for exactly
one #{x} in the #{j}-th coset when #{m=j}, and for no such
#{x} when #{m} and #{j} differ. The sum in \ref{fdip-013H}
therefore equals #{\mu s_j} when #{m} and #{j} differ, and it
equals #{\mu s_j+{\kappa}-{\mu}} when #{m=j}. The indicator
entry is #{\beta_m\beta_j} times this sum. Let #{D} be the
diagonal matrix with #{j}-th entry
#{\beta_j^2({\kappa}-{\mu})}, let #{p_j=\beta_j}, and let
#{c_j=\mu s_j\beta_j}. The entry from #{j} to #{m} equals the
corresponding entry of #{D+pc^{\top}}. \ref{fdip-013I}
applies once #{D} is invertible. \ref{fdip-012U} takes the
rescaling factors to be the positive square roots of the
coset sizes. \ref{fdip-012Y} assumes the Rayleigh inequality
for the rescaled matrix.}
}
